{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The men's tournament of the 2014 Asian Games Basketball Competition was held from September 20 to October 3, 2014 in Incheon, South Korea.","title":"Basketball at the 2014 Asian Games – Men"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" China was the defending Gold medal winners of the competition in 2010, which they won in their home soil.","title":"Basketball at the 2014 Asian Games – Men"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" South Korea emulated that feat by winning the Gold Medal in Incheon in 2014.","title":"Basketball at the 2014 Asian Games – Men"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Iran and Japan completed the podium by winning the Silver and Bronze medals, respectively.","title":"Basketball at the 2014 Asian Games – Men"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Games of the tournament were held at the 7,406 seat Samsan World Gymnasium, and the 5,158 seat Hwaseong Sports Complex.","title":"Basketball at the 2014 Asian Games – Men"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The 2006 Asian Games Opening Ceremony was held in Doha, Qatar on 1 December 2006 to mark the start of the 15th Asian Games.","title":"2006 Asian Games opening ceremony"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The ceremony took place in the multi-purpose Khalifa International Stadium, which was specifically renovated for the Asian Games.","title":"2006 Asian Games opening ceremony"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The ceremony was attended by over 50,000 spectators, as well as approximately 20,000 athletes and performers.","title":"2006 Asian Games opening ceremony"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Several Asian heads-of-state and the President of the International Olympic Committee Jacques Rogge were in attendance.","title":"2006 Asian Games opening ceremony"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The officials and organizers of the ceremony promised it to be the most spectacular opening ceremony in recent years.","title":"2006 Asian Games opening ceremony"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" The Opening Ceremony was described by the media to be one of the most breath-taking and technologically spectacular multi-sports event ceremony, and the most expensive multi-sports event ceremony (including both Opening and Closing ceremonies) in the history of Asian Games.","title":"2006 Asian Games opening ceremony"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Lebak Bulus Stadium was a multi-purpose stadium in Jakarta, Indonesia.","title":"Lebak Bulus Stadium"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The stadium had 12,000 seats and can be extended to 15,000 until 25,000 people.","title":"Lebak Bulus Stadium"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It was used mostly for football matches.","title":"Lebak Bulus Stadium"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The stadium held AFC U-17 Championship 2008 Qualification Group F Matches and Football Men's Tournament at the 2011 SEA Games Group B Matches.","title":"Lebak Bulus Stadium"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The 2010 Asian Para Games (officially known as the Guangzhou 2010 Asian Para Games) was a multi-sport event held in Guangzhou, China from December 12–19, 2010, in which athletes with physical disabilities competed against one another.","title":"2010 Asian Para Games medal table"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The Games replaced the FESPIC Games (Far East and South Pacific Games for the Disabled), and were held two weeks after the conclusion of the 16th Asian Games.","title":"2010 Asian Para Games medal table"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" A total of 2,512 athletes from 41 Asian National Paralympic Committees participated in the Games, competing in 19 sports.","title":"2010 Asian Para Games medal table"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" In the Games, 17 world and 82 Asian records were broken, compared with 3 world and 103 Asian records in the 2010 Asian Games.","title":"2010 Asian Para Games medal table"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Basketball was one of the 42 sports at the 16th Asian Games 2010 at Guangzhou, Guangdong, China.","title":"Basketball at the 2010 Asian Games"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The event was held at the 13,000 seat Guangzhou International Sports Arena, Huangpu Gymnasium, Guangti Gymnasium, Ying Dong Gymnasium and Sports and Entertainment Centre.","title":"Basketball at the 2010 Asian Games"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The 1974 Asian Games (officially known as the Seventh Asian Games) was a multi-sport event held in Tehran, Iran from September 1, 1974, to September 16, 1974.","title":"1974 Asian Games medal table"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" This was the first time that Asian Games were celebrated in any Middle East country.","title":"1974 Asian Games medal table"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" A total of 3,010 athletes selected from 25 Asian National Olympic Committees participated in 16 sports divided into 202 events.","title":"1974 Asian Games medal table"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The number of participating countries was the greatest in Asian Games history, eighteen nations competed in Bangkok, host of the 1970 Asian Games.","title":"1974 Asian Games medal table"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Fencing, gymnastics (artistic) and women's basketball were included for the first time; while sailing—which made its debut in the previous Asian Games—was not included, however since 1978, sailing is a part of the Asian Games sports.","title":"1974 Asian Games medal table"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Guangzhou International Sports Arena
